==== status
h2: High Availability Status
Real-time status of the HA cluster. Updates every 5 seconds.
h3: Cluster Status
| Cluster Overview
| Cluster State | HEALTHY
| Node Role | MASTER
| DHCP Server | dnsmasq (automatic)
| Instance: main | MASTER
| Instance: main_v6 | MASTER
| Last Synchronization
| Config Sync | <time>
| Lease Sync | <time>
h3: Service Status
| Service | Status | PID | Uptime
| keepalived | Running | <pid> | <uptime>
| owsync | Running | <pid> | <uptime>
| lease-sync | Running | <pid> | <uptime>
h3: Peer Status
| Peer Name | Address | State | Last Seen
| node2 | 172.30.0.11 | Online | <time>
==== simple
h2: High Availability - General
Configure a high availability cluster with 2 or more routers. Advanced options are available in the Advanced pages.
h3: Cluster Configuration
config.enabled [CBI.FlagValue] Enable HA Cluster: [x]
  help: Enable high availability clustering on this router
config.node_priority [CBI.Value] Priority: value=100 (placeholder "100")
  help: VRRP priority (1-255). Higher priority becomes MASTER.
config.vrrp_transport [CBI.ListValue] VRRP Transport: select=multicast {multicast,unicast}
  help: Multicast is the default. Use unicast when multicast is unavailable on the network. Unicast adverts go to the peers below, from the first Source Address set: this fits only failover groups on the peers' network; another group sets its own unicast addresses in Advanced VRRP.
config.firewall [CBI.FlagValue] Firewall Rules: [x]
  help: Keep the rules the cluster needs from its peers in the firewall configuration: VRRP on the interfaces of the instances and, from the addresses of the nodes, the ports of configuration sync, lease sync and the high availability of Kea, and ping. The rules are named HA-Cluster-… and show under Network → Firewall → Traffic Rules. When this is off they are removed and the firewall is yours to set up. Set it the same on every node.
h3: Peer Routers
Other routers of the cluster. Configuration and lease sync run with every peer not marked VRRP only. In multicast mode, VRRP does not use this list.
| Peer | Role | 
| peer1: node2 (172.30.0.11) | Cluster node | [☰][Edit][Delete]
<button> Add
h3: Virtual IP Addresses
All VIPs share a single failover group and switch together. Use the Advanced page to create independent failover groups. Multicast VRRP adds a second instance with VRID+128 for the IPv6 addresses. Unicast VRRP runs one instance in the family of the unicast addresses, and the other family fails over with it.
| Interface | VRID | IPv4 Address | IPv6 Address | Enable | 
| vip_lan: <img> lan | 51 | 192.168.50.254 | none | Yes | [☰][Edit][Delete]
| vip_vpn: <img> backend | 52 | 172.30.0.254 | none | Yes | [☰][Edit][Delete]
<button> Add VIP...
h3: Synchronization
Keeps configuration files and DHCP leases identical on the cluster nodes. These settings must be the same on every node.
config.sync_encryption [CBI.FlagValue] Encrypt Sync Traffic: [x]
  help: AES-256-GCM for configuration and lease sync. While this is on and there is a peer to sync with, the cluster does not start without a valid key. Switch it off only on a link protected otherwise, such as a VPN.
config.encryption_key [CBI.Value] Encryption Key: password=00112233445566778899aabbccddeeff00112233445566778899aabbccddeeff (placeholder "Click "Generate New Key" below to create a secure random key") button "∗"
  help: 256-bit AES key (64 hexadecimal characters), used for both configuration and lease sync. Must be identical on all nodes.
config._generate_key [CBI.ButtonValue] Generate New Key: button "Generate New Key"
h3: Configuration Files
config._group_dhcp [CBI.FlagValue] DHCP and DNS: [ ]
  help: /etc/config/dhcp: address pools, static leases, DNS settings.
config._group_firewall [CBI.FlagValue] Firewall: [ ]
  help: /etc/config/firewall: zones, rules, port forwards. Review the rules before enabling: they apply to every node.
h3: DHCP Leases
config.dhcp_backend [CBI.ListValue] DHCP Server: select=auto {auto,dnsmasq,kea}
  help: The server that gives out IPv4 leases on the cluster interfaces. Automatic: Kea once it is set up for a cluster interface and started (in the Kea pages), dnsmasq otherwise. Kea or dnsmasq: that one, and the HA cluster switches Kea's DHCPv4 server on or off itself. On a switch to Kea the static leases of dnsmasq are imported into Kea first, then the DHCPv4 of dnsmasq is switched off in the pools of the cluster interfaces (visible on the DHCP page), and switched on again on the way back; DNS and Router Advertisements stay with dnsmasq. With Kea, lease-sync does not run: Kea replicates its leases itself, through its own high availability settings, made in Kea on every node, and the IPv6 guard leaves DHCPv6 to Kea. Set the same on every node.
config.lease_sync [CBI.FlagValue] Real-time Lease Sync: [ ]
  help: Replicates DHCP leases between nodes so that clients keep their address after a failover. Independent of the DHCP configuration sync above.
==== simple modal peer1
h4: High Availability - General
peer1.name [CBI.Value] Peer Name: value=node2 (placeholder "router2")
peer1.address [CBI.Value] Peer IP Address: value=172.30.0.11 (placeholder "192.168.1.2")
peer1.source_address [CBI.ListValue] Source Address: select=172.30.0.10 {,172.30.0.10,fd00:172:30::10,192.168.50.10,fd00:192:168:50::10}
  help: Local IP to use when contacting this peer. Must match address family (IPv4/IPv6) of peer.
peer1.vrrp_only [CBI.FlagValue] VRRP only: [ ]
  help: Tick for a router that does not run ha-cluster, for example a non-OpenWrt VRRP router. It is excluded from configuration and lease sync. In multicast mode VRRP does not use peer entries, so this entry only feeds the Status page.
<button> Dismiss
